# Cloudflare Pages headers for numap.app. (GitHub Pages ignores this file.)
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS — post-deploy freeze:
# The app boots from a single hashed bundle referenced by index.html
# (<script type="module" src="./assets/index-<hash>.js">). Every deploy emits a
# new hash and deletes the old chunk. If a browser reuses a STALE index.html, it
# requests the deleted hash; the SPA fallback (`/* /index.html 200` in
# _redirects) then answers with the HTML shell — status 200, type text/html — in
# place of the JS. The browser refuses to execute HTML as a module, so the bundle
# never runs and the whole page is frozen until a hard refresh. The cure is to
# never let the HTML shell be served stale.

# Hashed build assets are content-addressed: a given filename never changes, so
# cache it forever. (Also stops the fallback ever mattering for a real asset.)
#
# ⚠ THE SHARP EDGE, verified 2026-08-05 by curl against a made-up hash:
# a MISSING /assets/ file is not a 404. The SPA fallback answers it, so the
# response is the HTML shell at status 200 — and this rule matches by PATH, so
# that HTML is served with `immutable, max-age=1y`. Two consequences, both of
# which bit us:
#   1. Status is not evidence. `r.ok` on a deleted chunk is true, which is why
#      the recovery screen used to announce the way back on its first probe
#      tick while the way back was still shut, and why the crash screen — which
#      waited for a 404 — never announced it at all. Detection now reads
#      assets/build.json (emitted by buildManifestPlugin in vite.config.js) and
#      requires a JS content-type: see index.html's checkLive.
#   2. The browser then pins HTML under a .js URL for a year. Content hashes
#      are deterministic, so a revert can resurrect a URL that was poisoned
#      under an older deploy — a page that reload() cannot fix and only a hard
#      refresh can. index.html's hardReturn re-fetches known-dead chunk URLs
#      with cache:'reload' to overwrite exactly those entries.
# `_redirects` cannot express a 404 (Pages supports only 200/301/302/303/307/
# 308), so the honest server-side fix would be a Pages Function on /assets/*
# turning a fallback-HTML response into a real 404 — not done: it puts a Worker
# invocation in front of every asset request.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# The HTML shell — served for / and every SPA route — must be revalidated on
# every load so a fresh deploy is picked up immediately and can never reference
# a chunk that no longer exists. no-cache = store but always revalidate (a 304
# keeps it cheap); it does NOT mean "don't store".
/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache
